1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,088
In 1975, this company invented the technology that would define the next 50 years.

2
00:00:06,088 --> 00:00:07,683
Then... they buried it.

3
00:00:07,683 --> 00:00:08,446
On purpose.

4
00:00:08,446 --> 00:00:11,775
This is the story of how Kodak destroyed itself.

5
00:00:12,375 --> 00:00:18,659
To understand how big a mistake this was, you need to understand how big this company actually was.

6
00:00:18,659 --> 00:00:22,658
At its peak, they controlled 90% of the film market in America.

7
00:00:22,658 --> 00:00:25,831
Their name was synonymous with photography itself.

8
00:00:25,831 --> 00:00:29,513
People didn't say take a picture, they said, Kodak moment.

9
00:00:30,113 --> 00:00:37,105
In 1975, a young engineer named Steve Sasson walked into his manager's office with a toaster-sized device.

10
00:00:37,105 --> 00:00:39,743
It was the world's first digital camera.

11
00:00:39,743 --> 00:00:41,261
His manager's response?

12
00:00:41,261 --> 00:00:44,163
That's cute, but don't tell anyone about it.

13
00:00:44,763 --> 00:00:48,581
For the next two decades, they kept making the same choice.

14
00:00:48,581 --> 00:00:49,875
They had the patent.

15
00:00:49,875 --> 00:00:51,428
They had the technology.

16
00:00:51,428 --> 00:00:58,870
But digital cameras threatened their film business, so they chose to protect the past instead of owning the future.

17
00:00:58,870 --> 00:01:02,236
By the time they tried to catch up, it was too late.

18
00:01:02,236 --> 00:01:04,500
In 2012, they filed for bankruptcy.

19
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:06,929
Here's what nobody tells you.

20
00:01:06,929 --> 00:01:10,018
Kodak didn't fail because they lacked innovation.

21
00:01:10,018 --> 00:01:14,052
They failed because they were too successful at the wrong thing.

22
00:01:14,052 --> 00:01:20,672
The same technology that could have saved them was invented in their own building, by their own employee.

23
00:01:20,672 --> 00:01:22,122
So here's the question.

24
00:01:22,122 --> 00:01:28,363
How many companies today are sitting on their own digital camera moment, and choosing to ignore it?
